    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d197">

                <pb facs="0321" n="245" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg245"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">651.116 Nitrate/80: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Straus</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-19T14:00:00+00:00">February 19, 1935—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 19—11 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>144. Reference Department’s telegram No. 60, February 18, 6 p.m. It is true that
                    Chile and Norway have been accorded entire nitrate quota and that United States
                    has been this morning refused participation. Embassy and Commercial Attaché in
                    particular have, however, been in almost daily communication with authorities
                    urging maintenance of our position under quota agreement of 1932.<anchor n="41"
                        xml:id="d197-f1"/> Inter-ministerial meeting to decide question is scheduled
                    for tomorrow. Although we are unofficially informed that Foreign Office is doing
                    its best to support our views and nitrate buying organization is favorably
                    inclined towards purchasing small tonnage, Agriculture appears in-alterably
                    opposed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Straus</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d197-f1" n="41" xml:id="d197-f1-content">
                <p>For the quota agreement of May 31, 1932, see telegram No. 342, May 31, 1932, from
                    the Ambassador in France, <ref target="frus1932v02#pg_232"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, 1932, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >ii</hi>, p. 232</ref>.</p>
            </note>
